## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key finding is that *Scientific American* published an opinion piece in October 2019 by Joel M. Moskowitz titled "We Have No Reason to Believe 5G Is Safe." This article expressed concerns and argued for a moratorium on 5G deployment. However, it was a contributed opinion piece, not an official editorial from the magazine. The magazine itself did not issue a definitive warning; instead, it published multiple perspectives on the topic and later planned to commission an objective review of the evidence, indicating no unified editorial stance declaring 5G unsafe.

The information we have is sufficient to evaluate the claim. The claim states that the magazine "warned" that 5G is not safe, which implies an official or definitive position. The evidence shows the critical view was presented as one author's opinion within a broader debate, not as a conclusive warning from the publication.

The claim is technically based on a real article but misleads by presenting an individual opinion piece as an official magazine warning, omitting the context that it was one side of a debate and not the magazine's endorsed conclusion. This selective presentation creates a significantly different impression than the full context provided by the evidence.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is misleading because it cherry-picks a contributed opinion piece by Joel M. Moskowitz, titled "We Have No Reason to Believe 5G Is Safe," and presents it as an official warning from *Scientific American*. In reality, the [article](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/scientific-american-created-confusion-5gs-safety-clear-joel-moskowitz) was an individual author's perspective published within a broader debate; the magazine itself hosted multiple viewpoints and later moved to commission an objective review, indicating no unified editorial stance against 5G safety. [Analyses](https://www.acsh.org/news/2019/10/21/scientific-american-says-5g-dangerous-vegetables-are-junk-food-14351) have criticized the piece for promoting fear without definitive evidence.